            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>Hobkins are eerie little creatures, pallid humanoids with enormous, lantern-like eyes and massive ears on a too-big head. Unlike other gremlins, hobkins aren't fully in step with the Universe. Instead, they're trapped halfway between it and the First World—eternally out of phase, just a hair's breadth from reality. They move strangely, with jerking, jittery gestures that jump between too fast and too slow, and they float above the ground. They have a physical form, but they feel off to the touch, with a texture not quite like anything else.</p>\n<p>Where most gremlins dismantle machines or relationships, hobkins love to scare people, striving to spread paranoia and terror wherever they go. A hobkins might lurk outside a child's window during a storm, pressing its great gleaming eyes against the glass until a lightning flash illuminates its horrible visage—and then vanish before the next lightning strike, giggling to itself with delight.</p><hr /><p>Like many fey, gremlins personify a natural force of the world: entropy. It's a fundamental truth that everything breaks, that every complex system, from a machine to a society, will inevitably grind itself down to nothing. Gremlins are happy to give those complex systems a friendly shove down the stairs.</p>\n<p>Nearly all gremlins live in the Universe, where they have fully acclimated to the plane. No one is quite certain why, but it seems likely that some long-forgotten gremlin managed to so irritate the Eldest to such a degree that their entire species was exiled—though it appears the Eldest missed a few, as some gremlins still lurk in the First World.</p>\n<p>Gremlins are usually found in an odd, parasitic relationship with mortal communities in the Universe. They don't need to eat or sleep, don't age or sicken, and never really die—but they do need to cause trouble. It's as essential to them as food and drink are to mortals, and nowhere do they get as many chances for chaos and confusion as in a mortal settlement. Gremlins thus become pests, living on the margins of society and bedeviling anyone and anything they can get their hands on.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
